".prawduct/changes/gitignore-contract-semantics.md", ".prawduct/changes/pre-dispatch-notice-one-owner.md", ".prawduct/changes/retire-chunk-status-shims.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-never-moves-head.md", ".prawduct/changes/test-evidence-no-stale-green.md", ".prawduct/changes/verify-coverage-stub-remedy.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/host-facts-2026-11-12.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The #1071 authorship test only counts files named by Edit/Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit, so work written through Bash or a subagent and committed between turns is still dropped, and the trace goes to stderr only", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `_drop_other_sessions_commits` judges authorship by `ToolActivity.edited_paths`, and `_TranscriptScan` fills that only from `EDIT_TOOLS` inputs. So 'which files did this session write' is answered by 'which files did its Edit-family calls name'. This is the shape gates.md warns about: 'A predicate a gate relies on must answer the GATE's question, not a nearby one'. Reachable case: a session writes code with Bash (`sed -i`, a heredoc, a formatter or codegen it runs) or through a Task/Agent subagent. Another session in the same checkout has written its transcript. The user commits the work from a terminal between turns. The commit is then idle, touches no Edit-named path, and is dropped. Neither the reflection gate nor the Critic trigger fires. Because the gates are disarmed, the Stop is clean, and at a clean Stop the `_LogOnlyAdvisory` trace goes only to the stderr log, which nobody reads. That is the one case where the trace matters. Search scope: every way a file in this checkout can change during the session without an Edit-family `file_path`. That includes Bash writes, subagent and sidechain edits, NotebookEdit-free notebook writes, and hook-run formatters. Expect members outside the diff. A fix needs a construction, not a longer `EDIT_TOOLS` table. One option: keep any idle commit whose paths were dirty in this checkout at one of this session's earlier Stops. `cmd_stop` already computes `status_output` every turn, and the activity cache could hold that set. This fails in the safe direction, since another session's uncommitted files only keep more. Also record each drop as a stop observation (`evidence.append_stop_observation`), so its rate can be audited without per-turn noise. Pin the change with a test of a Bash-written file committed from a terminal while a second transcript is live.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/host.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "describe_ignored_by_rule advises `!<dir>/**` for a committed directory, which git ignores when the hiding rule excludes the directory itself (e.g. a common `artifacts/` or `releases/` rule)", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (one renderer, `core.describe_ignored_by_rule`'s final `else` branch). Why it broke: that branch assumes the rule hides the files and not their directory. Take a plain basename directory rule such as `artifacts/`, `releases/` or `rules/`, which matches at any depth. `git check-ignore` reports it for `.prawduct/artifacts/prawduct-probe.md` through the parent directory. `_directory_rule('artifacts/')` returns `artifacts`, which is neither the entry nor its prefix, so the code falls to `!.prawduct/artifacts/**`. A trailing `/**` matches only what is inside the directory. Git does not re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so `.prawduct/artifacts/` stays hidden and the `contract-ignored-by-rule` advisory fires again after the owner pastes the advice. `artifacts/` and `releases/` are common build-output rules, and `.prawduct/artifacts/` is `always=True`, so consumer repos will hit this. The effect test (`test_the_advised_lines_leave_every_v2_path_unignored`) has only `*.md` as its non-directory rule, and `*.md` never excludes a directory, so the branch's failing case is never run. tests.md: 'An advisory's printed recommendation ships to every consumer repo \u2014 test its EFFECT'. Fix: for a directory entry, emit both `!<dir>/` (re-include the directory) and `!<dir>/**` (its contents, for a file-pattern rule). Then add `artifacts/` and a materialized `releases/` with `releases/` to that parametrize list, so git is asked about every path after the advice is applied.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py", "tests/test_gitignore_contract_semantics.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The suite remedy still has two composers: the verdict reason carries its own remedy, so every caller that appends suite_remedy prints it twice", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the record's state is classified and worded in two places, and the remedy is fused into the refusal string. `run_refusal` returns `record_in_progress`'s joined 'finding. Remedy' sentence (`_in_progress_parts` + `_cap(remedy)`), and its degraded branch writes a third remedy of its own ('Run the suite again, uncontended, and record that run'). `_load_test_evidence` passes that string up as `why_not`, so `tests_are_current` and `suite_vouches_for_tree` return it as their reason. The callers the change moved onto the 'one owner' then append `gates.suite_remedy(...).remedy` after that reason. In the running state the verify-resolutions exit-7 text (prawduct-hook, `{result['reason']}` then `{remedy}`), `check-releasability`'s `unproven-suite:` line and `transfer_remedy` (its lead embeds `tests_reason`, then 'A suite run is already under way: {suite_step.remedy}') each print 'Wait for it to record \u2014 `prawduct-hook test-status --wait` \u2026 do not start another run' twice. In the degraded state they print two differently worded remedies. `suite_remedy` also re-codes `run_refusal`'s precedence (in_progress, failed, degraded) by hand, and its docstring calls that agreement guaranteed ('so the two cannot disagree'). In fact it holds only by convention. The cause sits upstream of `test-report-contract.md`'s claim that 'the remedy comes from one owner \u2026 which every caller prints'. Construction: one classifier that returns (state, finding, remedy). `run_refusal` and the reason chain carry only the finding; `suite_remedy` returns the state and remedy from that same classification; `test-status`'s `running:` line joins them itself. Pin it with a test that the exit-7, unproven-suite and transfer-note texts each contain `WAIT_FOR_RUN` exactly once, with a degraded-state control.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The first-run void stub is a schema-valid run with made-up F4a values, so each reader has to work out 'this record carries no run' from several fields", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the cited rule 'When a field's ABSENCE carries meaning, a value NAMING the absence is its opposite' applies. The stub `_void_test_evidence` writes ('verifier': 'none (no record has landed)', 'coverage_level': 'referenced', empty lists, zero counts) passes the evidence schema as a run. Whether a record is a real run, a void over a run, a stub, or a void over a stub is not modelled in one place. Each reader rebuilds it from `void`, `void_counts_from`, `in_progress`, `degraded` and `verifier` presence. `run_refusal` and `suite_remedy` look at in_progress, failed and degraded. `_void_without_coverage` looks at void, void_counts_from and verifier. `_void_test_evidence` itself branches on already_void and void_counts_from. This bundle already produced one misreading: #1181, where verify-coverage read the stub's made-up F4a fields as a schema-bearing record and gave the wrong remedy. It was fixed with a special-case predicate, not by changing the model. That is more than 3 interdependent signals with no single source of truth, spread over several call sites. Construction: one `gates` classifier for the record's kind, which can share the classification the remedy finding asks for, and that every reader of `.test-evidence.json` asks. An alternative is to let the schema accept a void stub without F4a fields, so that absence stays absence. To bound the class, grep every direct reader of `.test-evidence.json` fields that does not go through `run_refusal` (verify_coverage, restamp/`--no-rerun`, flaky lines, store fallback).", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The census's `_review_chunks_label` re-implements `plan_status.fact_chunks`, which names itself the rule every reader of the field must use, and the two disagree on a malformed `chunks`", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: retiring the singular `chunk` sent the census to write its own chunks-or-chunk reader instead of asking the existing owner. `plan_status.fact_chunks`' docstring says it is 'the rule every reader of the field is to take from here'. The copy also differs from it: a non-list `chunks` makes `fact_chunks` answer [] but makes the census fall back to `chunk`. As a result chunk-status and the disposition census can name different chunks for one review. The next fact-shape change, like this one, then has a second reader to find. Replace the body with `', '.join(plan_status.fact_chunks(body)) or None`, or import lazily if a cycle forbids a top-level import.", "files": ["plugin/lib/dispositions.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A dropped commit's trace has no reader in exactly the case it exists for: a wrong drop disarms the gates, the Stop exits 0, and the line goes only to the stderr log", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (the one producer, cmd_stop's dropped-commit note). Why it broke: the trace was routed through `_LogOnlyAdvisory` to stop per-turn noise, but that class's own docstring says it has 'no reader at exit 0', and `_deliver_stop_advisories` says plain stderr at exit 0 'is logged and reaches nobody'. `session_work_span` now returns `dropped` 'so a wrong drop can be seen rather than read as \"this session changed nothing\"' (gates.py docstring; the change record says 'A dropped commit is not silent'). But a wrong drop removes the session's own commit from the span, so the reflection and Critic gates do not fire, so nothing blocks, so the Stop exits 0 and the note goes only to the hook log. The note reaches the model only when some unrelated gate happens to block. Nothing records it either: there is no fact, so `stats` cannot count drops or wrong drops. Consequence: when the drop predicate misfires, the session ends with no reflection and no review, and no person or query can see why. That happens when the user commits from a terminal while another session writes, and the commit holds files this session wrote with Bash (sed -i, a heredoc, a formatter, codegen) or through a delegate, whose Edit calls are in its own `subagents/` transcript. Core rule: 'A channel that is produced and never consumed is a DEFECT, not an inefficiency \u2014 name the consumer in the same change that adds the producer'. To fix: record each dropped commit once per sha through the existing durable sink (`evidence.append_stop_observation`, which `stats` already reads), or show it to the user once per sha through `host.emit_stop_advisory`. Either one keeps the no-repeat-per-turn property that motivated LogOnly.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Records Pass: the gate-span consumer note promises a terminal or `!git commit` of the session's edits is still asked, but only Edit/Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit targets count as the session's edits", "recommendation": "Bar: it ships. A `## Consumer note` becomes the release note. Scope: instance, the consumer note of `.prawduct/changes/gate-span-and-coverage-slips.md`. It says: 'A session that commits its own code is still asked, including when you commit its edits yourself from a terminal or with `!git commit`.' The code keeps such a commit only when one of its files is in `host.ToolActivity.edited_paths`. That set is built solely from the main transcript's `EDIT_TOOLS` calls (Edit, Write, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit; host.py). A file the session wrote through Bash is not in it. Neither is a file a delegate subagent edited, since that subagent's tool calls live in a separate `<session>/subagents/` transcript. If the user commits such a file from a terminal while a second session is writing in the checkout, the commit is dropped and the session is not asked. A consumer who reads the note trusts the gate in exactly that case. The code's own docstrings are precise ('touching no file this session's Edit/Write calls targeted'), so the fix is to the record: state the condition relationally ('...when the committed files are ones this session changed with its edit tools'). Or, if the code is widened to cover Bash or delegate writes, keep the note and pin it with a test. The same wording ('in files this one did not edit') appears in the subject file `plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md`.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/gate-span-and-coverage-slips.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/host.py"], "fid": "R-7"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "cmd_critic_begin's docstring still says `--chunk <id>` is a one-element alias kept for one release; the same function now refuses it", "recommendation": "Instance. plugin/bin/prawduct-hook line ~2198 says '`--chunk <id>` is its one-element alias, kept for one release; the two mix.' About 60 lines below, the code returns exit 1 for `--chunk`. Under the core rule 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees', delete the sentence; the critic skill was swept, this docstring was not. The begin_review docstring ('``chunk`` is its one-element alias for one release') describes a lib parameter that still exists, so it is stale as to timing only. No other edit is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "native-review record returns exit 1 rather than the idempotent 'already recorded' when the event exists but the session copy of the text is gone", "recommendation": "Instance, narrow. In `record`: if `ledger.native_review_event_exists` is true and the session's `.native-reviews/<id>.md` has been removed, the copy is skipped. The following `_read_text(path)` then raises, so the call returns 1 with 'the text could not be copied'. The docstring promises exit 0 when the review is already recorded. Either write the copy whenever the session file is missing, or check the event before the copy step.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "pr skill: on the fragment path, `legacy-pending-entry` reads only the committed log, but the prose says it also reads the on-disk log when git ignores it", "recommendation": "`coverage._committed_log_pending_verdict` reads `HEAD` only. The on-disk reading belongs to the untracked-log path (`_entry_check_without_history`). The new 'even on a branch that adds a valid fragment, the committed (or, when git ignores it, on-disk)' sentence in plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md is therefore broader than the code on the fragment path. Make the parenthetical relational, or drop it from that sentence.", "files": ["plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The briefing's 'may be another branch's' line does not fire when verify_anchor answers with a sibling's merge-base review", "recommendation": "`_this_branchs_record` asks `verify_anchor`, which keeps the owner's merge-base fallback. So the briefing shows a sibling's review anchored at the merge base as confirmed, with no doubt line. `settled_branch_review` exists to reject exactly that answer. The change record says that when no review of this branch can be confirmed, the briefing says it may be another branch's. This matches what the verify dispatch picks, so it is not a regression. Consider whether the display should ask `settled_branch_review` for its doubt line.", "files": ["plugin/lib/briefing.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "rule-unenforced: review/finding ids and review history in shipped comments ('#1178 R-4', 'R-2/R-7', 'R-8', '#848 R-4', 'Before #1178 R-4 this module classified the record itself\u2026')", "recommendation": "This is a class: 9 added sites in this delta, in gates.py (RUN_AND_RECORD comment, suite_remedy docstring), critic_consolidate.py (STALE_EVIDENCE_REASONS, which narrates history, and begin_review), record_progress.py (module docstring), prawduct-hook (cmd_critic_begin comment, `_void_test_evidence` docstring), release_readiness.py and evidence.py. Earlier reviews rated and accepted single instances (rev-20261110T135352Z R-7, R-4). The rule is written in review-protocol.md Severity Levels ('Review and finding ids, chunk numbers and review history never belong in a shipped comment'). The only tests (test_prose_severity_ceiling, test_v5_methodology) pin that the rule's text is present in the protocol. No check reads plugin/ code for it. To mechanise it, add a lint or test over plugin/lib and plugin/bin comments and docstrings that refuses a review-finding id (`\\bR-\\d+\\b`, `rev-\\d{8}T`) and a 'Before #NNN' history clause. Delete the instances when that check lands.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/record_progress.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "read-cost: the session handoff now reads the whole fact store a second time", "recommendation": "`_this_branchs_record` calls `evidence.read_facts` for every handoff whose findings cache names a fact. When a plan is bound, `render_session_handoff` reads the store again for `plan_status.chunk_states`. The module already has a read-once idiom (`_read_facts_once`). Thread one read through both callers. No volume is stated, but the store grows with every review (see `templates/data-model.md` 'Access Paths').", "files": ["plugin/lib/briefing.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "record_progress memoises liveness probes in a process-global dict that only an explicit forget() clears", "recommendation": "`_PROBED` is keyed by (pid, started_at) and lives for the whole process. Correctness depends on every long-lived in-process caller remembering to call `forget()`. Today the only such caller is `test-status --wait`. A future polling caller that does not call it would keep reading a dead recorder as running. Consider scoping the memo to one verdict (pass a probe cache down from the reader, or memoise inside `suite_remedy`/`tests_are_current`'s call) so that forgetting it is impossible rather than a convention.", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_progress.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "check_change_log_entry holds a verdict function's output back with contextlib.redirect_stdout", "recommendation": "`_fragment_entry_verdict` prints its verdict as a side effect, and the caller now captures stdout globally so it can drop or replay that output. Having the verdict return its line(s) with the exit code would remove the redirect and make the ordering explicit. Low priority: it works as written.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "critic_mode's _UNSET sentinel keeps an optional-recompute path alive only for one direct test call", "recommendation": "`infer_mode` is the only production caller of `_rule_verify_resolutions_fires` and `_rule_postfix_fix_fires`, and it always passes `prior_record`. The `_UNSET` default is used only by `test_critic_mode_inference.py`'s direct call. Making the argument required and having that test pass `_verify_prior_record(...)` would remove the sentinel and the `Any` import.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The wrapper prompt hardcodes the review text's path instead of deriving it from native_review.own_text_path", "recommendation": "`native_review_prompt` writes the literal `.prawduct/.native-reviews/{review_id}.md`, while `own_text_path` and `DIRNAME` own that path and `prepare` prints the real one. If they diverge, the wrapper gets two conflicting instructions and `record` reports the text as unwritten. Render the relative form from `native_review.DIRNAME` (it is already imported there), or say only 'the path prepare printed'.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The doctor skill's Health-Check #8 restates core.V2_COMMITTED_PATHS in prose, and the copy is already incomplete", "recommendation": "The doctor skill's Health-Check #8 lists the committed set but omits `change-log.md`, `change-log-archive/` and `owner-rulings.md`. The doctor only relays the lines `update-gitignore --dry-run` prints, so the list drives nothing. Delete the enumeration and keep the pointer to the constant. Separately, under Framework Check 9 (Cumulative Health), check #8 is now one paragraph of about 400 words with the grading rule, remedy and scope all inline. Consider splitting its decision points into short bullets.", "files": ["plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "gates._other_session_activity returns THIS session's activity", "recommendation": "The name reads as another session's activity, but the function returns this session's `ToolActivity`, gated on another session having written. A name such as `_own_activity_if_shared` would match what callers get.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "rule-unenforced: 'Review and finding ids ... never belong in a shipped comment' has no check, and this delta adds ~9 instances", "recommendation": "review-protocol.md (Severity Levels, Prose remedies) says review and finding ids and review history never belong in a shipped comment, and a comment narrating history is a deletion finding. `tests/test_prose_severity_ceiling.py` pins only that the protocol still states the ban; nothing scans shipped code, and record_lint's CHECKS cover records only. This delta adds `(#1178 R-4)` in gates.py (twice: the RUN_AND_RECORD comment and the suite_remedy docstring), release_readiness.py, prawduct-hook (twice) and critic_consolidate.py. It also adds `(#1178 R-8)` in prawduct-hook `_void_test_evidence`, `(#1178 R-2/R-7)` plus 'Without the bound, a recorder ... read as running forever' in record_progress.py's module docstring, and 'Before #1178 R-4 this module classified the record itself...' in critic_consolidate.py's STALE_EVIDENCE_REASONS comment. Prior reviews filed this class as notes (rev-20261110T135352Z R-4, R-7), so it recurs. Rated as an instance would be: prose that changes no one's action. Mechanising it would take a lint over the lines a diff adds under `plugin/**` that flags a finding-id token (`R-\\d+`) or a `rev-` review id inside a comment or docstring, wired the way record_lint's added-lines reader already works for records. No per-site edit is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/record_progress.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-13"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Records Pass: 'Every remedy comes from one owner' overstates the transfer note, which takes suite_remedy's clause only in the running state", "recommendation": "`.prawduct/changes/test-evidence-no-stale-green.md` says both gates' coverage-transfer note prints `gates.suite_remedy` 'instead of each adding its own \"run the suite\"'. `gates.transfer_remedy` uses `suite_step.remedy` only when `state == SUITE_RUNNING`. For interrupted, failing, degraded and not-current it still prints its own 'Run the suite and `prawduct-hook test-evidence record`', so the failing arm ('fix the failing test(s), then ...') and the degraded arm ('..., uncontended') never reach it. Clears no bar: the advice it prints is not wrong enough to send anyone astray. Noted because a maintainer who trusts 'one owner' and rewords suite_remedy will find the transfer note does not follow. Either let the non-running arms take `suite_step.remedy`, or make the sentence relational ('a run in progress is told to wait by the one owner').", "files": [".prawduct/changes/test-evidence-no-stale-green.md", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-14"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Records Pass coverage: all 14 files_oracle read and rated", "recommendation": "Covered: .claude/rules/learnings/gates.md and tests.md; .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md and data-model.md (the native-review prepare/record text, the `--chunk` usage error, and the exit-7 slug set `running|interrupted|failing|degraded|not-current` all match gates.SUITE_STATES and the hook); the change records branch-review-one-owner, gate-span-and-coverage-slips, gitignore-contract-semantics, pre-dispatch-notice-one-owner, retire-chunk-status-shims, review-never-moves-head, test-evidence-no-stale-green and verify-coverage-stub-remedy; .prawduct/project-state.yaml (norm_health 2026-11-16 entry); and .prawduct/research/native-review-paired/host-facts-2026-11-12.md. Checked against code: 8-transcript cache and 256-byte prefix check (host.py), the 3x / 30-minute unverified bound (record_progress.py), `_emit_pre_dispatch_notice` routing, `settled_